    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup for the Austin Waves is anchored by **Kyle Tucker** (82.70) and **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (74.56), both of whom capitalize on high-upside matchups against Ober. **Kyle Tucker** remains our premier offensive engine, benefiting from a soft SP_xERA of 4.82 and a strong batting order placement. Similarly, **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** finds himself in an ideal spot; his score is inflated by a significant 19% SP skill advantage and a favorable platoon profile against right-handed pitching. **Heliot Ramos** (67.76) rounds out the high-efficiency tier, specifically targeted for his ability to exploit Ober\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a tangible boost to his stolen base probability in a category where we remain hyper-aggressive.\n\nStrategic adjustments were required to navigate a crowded utility group, with **Coby Mayo** (53.21) serving as our most difficult bench decision. While **Coby Mayo** provided a solid score, he fell just short of the efficiency threshold established by **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (58.46) and **Josh Jung** (59.90). We are keeping a close watch on **Gunnar Henderson** (50.12), whose score remains suppressed by a challenging matchup against Manaea; the combination of a difficult left-left platoon and Manaea's ability to hold runners makes him a high-risk asset today, though he retains his spot due to his daily floor. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus as we approach the final stretch of the season. **Wade Meckler** (43.44) and **Sam Antonacci** (43.53) are currently hovering near our \"marginal\" floor; **Sam Antonacci** specifically suffers from a negative platoon impact against Griffin, and while the wind blowing out offers some protection for his power numbers, his efficiency is tenuous. We have cleared our bench of non-starters, with **Nasim Nunez** (38.94) benched as a safety measure to preserve game caps, given the restrictive impact of the opposing catcher's pop time and the low spot in the order. Owners should monitor the status of **Henry Bolte** (44.78), as his production is highly dependent on his leadoff role; any late shifts in the batting order could necessitate a pivot to ensure we maximize our category coverage.",
